# API origin the /api/v<N>/** pass-through (rewrites in next.config.ts) forwards to — scheme +
# host (+ port), no path or trailing slash, e.g. https://api.example.com or, locally, a
# go-scaffold API on http://localhost:8090. Server-only: never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_ (that
# would ship the API's address to the browser). It is BUILD-TIME: Next bakes a rewrite's
# destination into the build, so set it where `pnpm build` / `pnpm preview` / `pnpm run deploy`
# run (this file, or the CI environment) — a Worker variable set later changes nothing.
# Unset, `next build` and `next dev` fail with a message naming it.
API_ORIGIN=
# The pass-through forwards no X-Forwarded-For / CF-Connecting-IP, so the API sees this
# app's egress address as the client: every browser user shares one rate-limit bucket.
# Do not set TRUSTED_PROXY on the API to "fix" that — a visitor-supplied header would be
# trusted. The real fix is forwarding the visitor IP in a header the API is configured to
# trust, with the API origin locked to this app (needs a go-scaffold change).
# On the API, set WEB_ORIGINS to this app's origin (cookie CSRF check), and for local http
# dev either run the app over HTTPS or set the API's SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false.
